{"name":"Old English White","hex":"#f4f8dd","fetched":"2026-10-24","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"old","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mochi."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, concept, relationship, etc., having existed for a relatively long period of time.","Of a living being, having lived for most of the expected years."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/old"},{"term":"english","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Spinning or rotary motion given to a ball around the vertical axis, as in pool, billiards or bowling; spin, sidespin.","An unusual or unexpected interpretation of a text or idea, a spin, a nuance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative form of English."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/english"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"American Bulldog","blocks":[{"text":"The American Bulldog is a large, muscular breed of mastiff-type dog. Their ancestors were brought to the British North American colonies where they worked on small farms and ranches."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/American_Bulldog_600.jpg/330px-American_Bulldog_600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Bulldog","wikidata":"Q37604"},{"title":"Old English","blocks":[{"text":"Old English, Early English or Anglo-Saxon, is the earliest attested form of the English language, spoken in England and southern and eastern Scotland in the Early Middle Ages. It developed from the languages brought to Great Britain by Anglo-Saxon settlers in the mid-5th century, and the first Old English literature dates from the mid-7th century. After the Norman Conquest of 1066, English was replaced as the language of the upper classes for several centuries by Anglo-Norman, a type of French. 1066 is therefore regarded as marking the end of the Old English era, since during the subsequent period the English language was heavily influenced by Anglo-Norman and Old Norse, developing into what is now known as Middle English."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_English","wikidata":"Q42365"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/American_Bulldog_600.jpg/500px-American_Bulldog_600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/American_Bulldog_600.jpg/500px-American_Bulldog_600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmerican_Bulldog_600.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Philip_Reinagle_-_Bulldog.jpg/330px-Philip_Reinagle